Ladybird picks the font of each character of canvas text by the
character’s emoji presentation, and keeps an emoji sequence in one font.
Blink does the same: BaseRenderingContext2D::DrawTextInternal() shapes
canvas text with the same shaper and font fallback as other text. So
do WebKit, in CanvasRenderingContext2D::drawTextInternal(), and Gecko,
whose CanvasBidiProcessor makes its text runs through gfxFontGroup.

WordPress draws four emoji sequences, and each of them again with zero
width spaces in it, and takes a sequence as supported where the two
drawings differ. In Chrome 157.0.8103.79, Firefox 151.0, and WebKit
26.5, all four differ, as they do in Ladybird. The new test prints the
same lines in the three engines as in Ladybird, with two exceptions:
WebKit draws the color glyph for an emoji with a text variation selector
where the color font is listed first. And in Firefox, a letter that
follows an emoji and a joiner isn’t as wide as an Ahem glyph.

After a joiner, a character whose variation selector asks for its text
presentation gets its font by that presentation, rather than staying
in the emoji font: UTS #51 allows no text presentation sequence in an
emoji ZWJ sequence. That rule comes from the specification alone, since
the fonts at hand show no difference between the two in any engine. A
joiner that follows a character that’s no emoji joins nothing either:
The character after it gets its font from the cascade, as any other
character does. The lines the test has for that case print the same in
the three engines as in Ladybird.
